  "worked_solution": "The kinetic energy $K$ is directly proportional to the square of the velocity $v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nK=k{v}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that $K=100\\,\\text{J}$ when $v=5\\,\\text{m/s}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & =k\\cdot {5}^{2} \\\\ 100 & =k\\cdot 25 \\\\ \\frac{100}{25} & =k \\\\ 4 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the constant of variation is $4.$",
